{% doc %}
  Resolves block-level typography and colour settings into CSS custom properties.

  ## Why this is a snippet and not copy-pasted into each block

  `heading` and `text` need the same override machinery, and any block added
  later (`_product-title`, `announcement`, `testimonial`) will need it too.
  Duplicating twenty lines of `case` statements per block is how the keyword
  mappings drift apart — a "loose" line height meaning 1.9 in one block and 1.8
  in another is the kind of bug nobody reports and everybody notices.

  ## The override contract

  Every property is emitted **only when the merchant overrode it**. A block that
  leaves `type_preset` on `inherit` emits nothing at all, so the element falls
  through to the global type scale from `css-variables.liquid`. That is the
  point: theme settings stay the single source of truth, and a block override is
  a deliberate exception rather than a silent shadow copy.

  This is also why the values are custom properties rather than direct
  declarations. `font-size: 2.4rem` inline beats every stylesheet including the
  mobile media query; `--block-font-size: 2.4rem` is read by a rule in
  `base.css` that can still swap it out below 750px.

  ## Colour and gradient are mutually exclusive

  Gradient text needs `background-image` plus `color: transparent` set together —
  one without the other is invisible text. So when a gradient is chosen the
  solid colour is not emitted at all, and the `text-gradient` class in
  `base.css` owns both halves. The schema enforces the same thing with
  `visible_if`, but a merchant who set a colour *before* switching to gradient
  still has that value stored, and this guard is what stops it applying.

  Parameters:
    block    (object, required)  `block.settings`
    fallback (string, optional)  CSS var name for the unoverridden size,
                                 e.g. 'var(--font-size-h2)'. Emitted as
                                 `--block-font-size` only when no override.

  Setting ids read:
    type_preset, font_family, font_weight, font_size, font_size_mobile,
    line_height, letter_spacing, text_transform, text_color, gradient

  Returns: a semicolon-terminated declaration list, no `style=` wrapper, so the
  caller can concatenate it with its own properties.

  @example
    <h2 class="block-heading__text"
        style="{% render 'block-typography', block: block.settings %}">
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign out = ''

  # ---- Typography overrides ------------------------------------------------
  # Gated as a group. A merchant on `inherit` gets nothing emitted, which is
  # what lets the global scale keep working.

  if block.type_preset == 'custom'

    case block.font_family
      when 'heading'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-font-family:var(--font-heading-family);'
      when 'accent'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-font-family:var(--font-accent-family);'
      when 'body'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-font-family:var(--font-body-family);'
    endcase

    if block.font_weight != blank and block.font_weight != 'inherit'
      assign out = out | append: '--block-font-weight:' | append: block.font_weight | append: ';'
    endif

    comment
      Sizes are stored in px because that is what a merchant thinks in, and
      emitted in rem against the 62.5% root so a customer who enlarged their
      browser text keeps that preference. Same conversion as css-variables.
    endcomment
    if block.font_size != blank
      assign size_rem = block.font_size | divided_by: 10.0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-font-size:' | append: size_rem | append: 'rem;'
    endif

    comment
      Mobile falls back to the desktop value rather than to the global scale.
      A merchant who set 72px desktop and left mobile alone wants 72px, not the
      theme's h2 — surprising them with a third value is worse than being large.
    endcomment
    if block.font_size_mobile != blank
      assign size_mobile_rem = block.font_size_mobile | divided_by: 10.0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-font-size-mobile:' | append: size_mobile_rem | append: 'rem;'
    elsif block.font_size != blank
      assign out = out | append: '--block-font-size-mobile:' | append: size_rem | append: 'rem;'
    endif

    comment
      Keyword to number, matching the maps in css-variables.liquid exactly.
      If those change, change these in the same commit.
    endcomment
    case block.line_height
      when 'tight'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-line-height:1.15;'
      when 'normal'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-line-height:1.6;'
      when 'loose'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-line-height:1.9;'
    endcase

    case block.letter_spacing
      when 'tight'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-letter-spacing:-0.02em;'
      when 'normal'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-letter-spacing:0em;'
      when 'wide'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-letter-spacing:0.06em;'
    endcase

    if block.text_transform != blank and block.text_transform != 'inherit'
      assign out = out | append: '--block-text-transform:' | append: block.text_transform | append: ';'
    endif
  endif

  # ---- Colour --------------------------------------------------------------
  # Deliberately outside the `type_preset` gate. Wanting one heading in the
  # accent colour is a common, reasonable ask that should not require opting
  # into a full typography override first.

  assign gradient = block.gradient | default: 'none'

  if gradient == 'none' or gradient == blank
    case block.text_color
      when 'text'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-text-color:var(--color-text);'
      when 'subtext'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-text-color:var(--color-subtext);'
      when 'heading'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-text-color:var(--color-heading);'
      when 'accent'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-text-color:var(--color-accent-text);'
      when 'sale'
        assign out = out | append: '--block-text-color:var(--color-sale-price);'
    endcase
  endif

  # ---- Unoverridden size fallback ------------------------------------------
  # So base.css can read one property instead of nesting three var() fallbacks.

  if block.type_preset != 'custom' or block.font_size == blank
    if fallback != blank
      assign out = out | append: '--block-font-size:' | append: fallback | append: ';'
      assign out = out | append: '--block-font-size-mobile:' | append: fallback | append: ';'
    endif
  endif
-%}

{{- out -}}
